body:
Canvas rematerialization can reject a valid final layout because a newly positioned Y-axis label is tested against the previous numeric title graphic.

Reproduced with the exact unpublished candidate c2c6cdfba1df97d085f55a4da19878da55c07412 (inherited manifest0.0.23; no published-registry claim):

```js
const title = {text: "Y", fontSize: 11, fontWeight: 700, offset: 35.7412109375};
const p = chart()
  .createCanvas({plot: {width: 100, height: 100}, margin: {left: 58.5, top: 40, right: 79.25, bottom: 48.5}})
  .createData({values: [{x:0,y:-5}, {x:1,y:0}, {x:2,y:5}]})
  .createScatterPlot({x: {field:"x", scale:{domain:[0,2],nice:false,zero:false}}, y: {field:"y", scale:{domain:[-5,5],nice:false,zero:false}}, guides:false})
  .createYAxis({ticksAndLabels: {values:[-5,0,5], ticks:{length:5}, labels:{fontSize:10,format:".1f",offset:6.5}}, title});
p.editCanvas({margin:{left:47,top:40,right:7,bottom:37}});
// Current error: The y-axis labels overlap the axis title.
```

The final label bounds are x22.2..40.5. The title should move from x17.2587890625..28.2587890625 to x5.7587890625..16.7587890625, leaving a valid gap. The current replay compares against the earlier title. Removing the title, applying the same Canvas edit, then recreating the identical numeric title succeeds. That is diagnostic evidence, not a proposed authoring workaround. The original program remains unchanged after failure.

Expected: validate the final prospective sibling geometry while preserving existing overlap/Canvas/owner checks, numeric offset meaning, failure atomicity and immutable input. No tolerance increase, hidden title, font alteration or new public API is requested. Add shrink/expand, X/Y/named-owner and genuine-overlap rejection regressions.

Implementation is pending; the current label-relative offset feature has a separate issue #267 and will finish its coherent checkpoint first.

## Problem

A fixed-frame reference can intentionally contain overlapping axis text. Cartesian labels already accept `overlap: 'allow'` for label-label collisions, but an axis title always rejects label-title collisions. Faithful static reproduction currently needs unsupported title ownership or altered source text/geometry.

Reference specimen0107 has 150 tick rectangles in a 300×81 plot /668×160 frame. All150 paints match a public Rug+static-highlight program. Its original Y title bbox is `[147.5,33.015625,160.5,106.984375]`, intersecting three original label bboxes. With `labels.overlap:'allow'`, the installed candidate953679ba rejects `createYAxisTitle`: `The y-axis title overlaps the axis labels.` This is an opt-in capability gap, not a request to weaken default layout checks.

## Reproduction

```js
import { chart } from 'ggaction';

chart()
  .createCanvas({
    width: 500, height: 220,
    margin: { left: 320, right: 20, top: 20, bottom: 60 }
  })
  .createData({ values: Array.from({ length: 10 }, (_, i) => ({
    rank: i + 1, group: 'An intentionally long category ' + (i + 1)
  })) })
  .createScatterPlot({
    x: 'rank', y: { field: 'group', fieldType: 'nominal' }, guides: false
  })
  .createAxes({
    x: false,
    y: {
      ticksAndLabels: { labels: { fontSize: 14, overlap: 'allow' } },
      title: { text: 'Group', fontSize: 11, offset: 80 }
    }
  });
```

Reproduced on the immutable local953679ba package. No reference image pixels were inspected; original SVG text geometry and native error diagnoses were checked. No model request was used.

## Proposed existing-API extension

Add `overlap?: 'error' | 'allow'` to Cartesian `AxisTitleOptions`, including complete axes and owned title editors. Omission remains strict. `'allow'` suppresses only label-title collision rejection for that title; label-label overlap and Canvas bounds remain separate checks. Apply the same owner policy when labels are created/edited after a title. Keep named-axis, facet, rematerialization, persistence and Basic/Full behavior consistent. Reject invalid values atomically, and allow an explicit reset to `'error'` only when the resulting layout is valid.

No new action, renderer primitive, global geometric epsilon, font shrinking, data change or default output change. Title Canvas overflow stays an error.

## Verification and release

- Primitive/public exact same-run decoded output for the approved overlap target.
- Default strict output/error behavior, both edit orders, reset/rollback, named owners, facets, scale/Canvas/data rematerialization, persistence validation, public types and installed package consumers.
- Synchronized current contract, canonical action/type references, search and generated docs.
- Full current chart-cohort native compatibility before local adoption.

Standing user authorization explicitly approves all in-scope future design questions. Preserve the 200-success-family public release cadence: public1213; next regular checkpoint1413. Keep this issue open until actual public package/docs/dataset verification. Specimen0107 stays unadmitted with zero model requests until its full native/source qualification succeeds.

## Problem

Basic field encoding reassignment calls `program.editScale`, which is intentionally absent from the Basic public API. An existing ordinal dash mapping cannot replace its scale range even though the field encoding action is available.

Confirmed before the categorical dash fallback change, using the installed unreleased `49adee7d23e3651000ccb425fbf2995d5a163348` candidate (package manifest `0.0.21`); reproduced again in the working source. This is a pre-existing gap, separate from #241. Registry-package behavior has not been claimed or verified here.

## Public reproduction

```js
import { chart } from 'ggaction/basic';

const program = chart()
  .createCanvas()
  .createData({values: [
    {x: 0, y: 1, group: 'a'},
    {x: 1, y: 2, group: 'b'}
  ]})
  .createLinePlot({x: 'x', y: 'y', guides: false})
  .encodeStrokeDash({field: 'group'});

program.encodeStrokeDash({
  field: 'group',
  scale: {range: ['dotted']}
});
```

Actual: `TypeError: program.editScale is not a function` from the shared encoding-scale application path.

Expected: supported Basic encoding reassignment should update and rematerialize the bound scale through an internally available wrapped action. Preserve the Basic public method boundary, immutable input/trace and shared-consumer atomicity; do not add the Full public editor family to Basic as a workaround. Audit other field-encoding channels using the same path.

## Tracking and delivery

Recorded while validating #241. Its installed-package tests separately cover Basic first construction and Full editing/reassignment; this issue is not represented as fixed. Keep the current chart translation sequential and preserve existing completed chart epochs. Implement and verify as a separate coherent change; public integration remains the next 200-family checkpoint at 1,413 successful families.

## Problem

An omitted right-side legend offset ignores an existing right Y axis. A chart with ample right margin is rejected when `createLegend({})` puts the legend into that axis lane. The symmetric left-side case needs the same default placement rule.

Reproduced with the published `ggaction@0.0.21` artifact at `e93500081b6dde6be7614e3e9352e947bec5908d`:

```js
import { chart } from 'ggaction';

const p = chart()
  .createCanvas({
    plot: { width: 300, height: 200 },
    margin: { left: 40, right: 220, top: 40, bottom: 40 }
  })
  .createData({ values: [
    { x: 1, y: 1, category: 'A' },
    { x: 2, y: 2, category: 'B' }
  ] })
  .createScatterPlot({ x: 'x', y: 'y', guides: false })
  .encodeColor({ field: 'category' })
  .createAxes({
    x: false,
    y: [{ id: 'rightAxis', scale: 'y', position: 'right' }]
  });

p.createLegend({});
// The right rightAxis-axis labels and color legend overlap;
// guides require more margin space or different offsets.

p.createLegend({ offset: 90 }); // Builds with the same Canvas/data/axes.
```

A complete source chart with 471 original rows, two Y scales and a 576×364 Canvas reproduces this before any model request. Its raw nested metadata can already be stored by the library; this failure is separate from the translation input validator.

## Expected behavior / proposed scope

Extend the existing inferred top/bottom axis-clearance policy to omitted external left/right offsets. Use actual same-edge axis line/ticks/labels/title occupied bounds, preserve the family default gap and the plot-top vertical anchor, and retain the stored offset plus existing `inferredOffset` intent.

Explicit offsets, free positions, in-plot corners and legacy layouts keep their authored anchors and normal collision checks. Do not expand the Canvas, change data/scales, hide labels, or introduce a new public action/parameter. Preserve order-independent replay for axis edits/removal, Canvas changes, legend groups and persistence; outputs without a relevant side axis must remain unchanged.

## Validation

Cover the minimal right-axis case and its left mirror; occupied borders/strokes; categorical, continuous, size and multi-block legends; explicit-offset rejection/immutability; axis-before-legend versus legend-before-axis; axis editing/removal and persistence; Full/Basic installed consumers; and existing default chart output compatibility.

The user's standing in-scope design approval applies. Implementation will stay in the tested batch candidate and will not be published before the next 200-family release checkpoint.

## Problem

`encodeStrokeDash` accepts only `fieldType: "nominal"`. An ordered categorical field cannot retain its `"ordinal"` meaning, even with a complete explicit domain and valid dash range. The error currently refers to a color field:

```js
import { chart } from "ggaction";

const program = chart()
  .createCanvas({ width: 260, height: 180, margin: 24 })
  .createData({ values: [
    { level: 2, x: 0, y: 20 }, { level: 2, x: 1, y: 20 },
    { level: 1, x: 0, y: 10 }, { level: 1, x: 1, y: 10 }
  ] })
  .createLinePlot({ x: "x", y: "y", guides: false });

program.encodeStrokeDash({
  field: "level",
  fieldType: "ordinal",
  scale: { domain: [1, 2], range: [[1, 0], [4, 2]] }
});
// Error: Unsupported color field type "ordinal".
```

This blocks an ordered-category comparison during chart translation. No paid generation was dispatched for that source, and the previous immutable program remains unchanged after rejection. The current public declaration is nominal-only as well; this is a missing categorical capability rather than a documentation retrieval failure.

## Proposed existing-API extension

- Accept `"nominal" | "ordinal"` in the existing field mode; keep nominal as the omitted default.
- Preserve the exact type in semantic state. Reuse the existing categorical validator and ordinal dash mapping.
- Keep automatic domain order at first appearance; explicit domain order wins. Do not sort rows or silently coerce category values.
- Use the same behavior through line/rule, existing line facade and bulk channel assignment.
- Preserve path identity, one raw appearance value per series, missing-value policy, scale edits, legends, selections and atomic rejection.
- Keep constant-mode restrictions and direct/named pattern validation, including valid zero-gap patterns, unchanged.

## Verification

Cover semantic intent, explicit/automatic order, early/late assignment, line/rule/facade/bulk paths, scale and constant reassignment, legend/selection rematerialization, persistence and render exports. Matching explicit nominal/ordinal domain/range must produce the same SVG and decoded pixels. Invalid types and conflicting per-series values must remain immutable failures. Synchronize types, current contract, generated action cards/references and public docs.

The user has already approved in-scope future API designs. Implementation will be collected in the tested candidate branch and integrated with the next regular 200-completed-chart release; this issue remains open until integration.

## Problem

In published ggaction 0.0.21, shared facet legend inference combines continuous color and continuous stroke as an ordinal legend. Both individual continuous encodings and their explicit gradient owners already work. No HCL interpolation is involved in this reproduction.

```javascript
import { chart } from "ggaction";
const scale = { domain: [0, 1], range: ["red", "blue"], interpolate: "lab", whitePoint: "d50" };
const base = chart()
  .createCanvas({ width: 800, height: 800, margin: 200 })
  .createData({ values: [
    { x: 0, y: 0, v: 0, g: "a" },
    { x: 1, y: 1, v: 1, g: "b" }
  ] })
  .createScatterPlot({ x: "x", y: "y", guides: false })
  .encodeColor({ field: "v", fieldType: "quantitative", scale })
  .encodeStroke({ field: "v", fieldType: "quantitative", scale });

base.facet({ field: "g", guides: { legend: "shared" } });
// Error: Legend requires resolved ordinal scale "color".

// Existing supported flow succeeds with unchanged data:
base.createLegend({ title: false })
  .facet({ field: "g", guides: { legend: "shared" } });
```

## Expected behavior

Shared legend inference should preserve the two continuous scale owners and produce their continuous legends, consistent with explicit `createLegend` before faceting. It must not reinterpret the quantitative fields as categorical or change the original rows, scale domains, interpolation or paint values. Preserve existing ordinal color/stroke combination behavior.

## Diagnosis and scope

The failing route passes through `prepareAutoLegendSource` / `prepareSharedFacetLegend` into the categorical legend creation plan. The same program with explicit continuous owners succeeds. The earlier immutable program and both original rows remain unchanged after failure.

Reproduced independently against the installed, byte-verified published 0.0.21 package. Found while checking neutral HCL consumers for #227, then reproduced with Lab to establish that this is an independent pre-existing defect. An existing explicit-owner flow avoids the error, so it does not block unrelated chart translation or the neutral-hue fix. No image pixels inspected and no new model request was required.

## Observed behavior

In `ggaction@0.0.19`, HCL interpolation toward an equal-channel neutral gray rotates toward an arbitrary hue introduced by the RGB→Lab matrix residual. A blue→near-white ramp consequently becomes purple near its middle. Setting the newly supported D50 convention does not resolve this achromatic endpoint case.

```js
import { chart } from 'ggaction';
const p = chart().createCanvas({width:300,height:180})
  .createData({values:[{x:0,y:0,v:0.5}]})
  .createScatterPlot({x:'x',y:'y',guides:false})
  .encodeColor({field:'v',fieldType:'quantitative',scale:{
    domain:[0,1], range:['#4682b4','#fdfdfd'],
    interpolate:'hcl', whitePoint:'d50'
  }});
```

For a neutral endpoint the hue is undefined; retaining the chromatic endpoint's hue makes this segment equivalent to a straight Lab segment. Current `rgbToHcl` always assigns a finite `atan2` hue, and `mix` always interpolates it, even for equal-channel neutral RGB inputs. Both D50 and D65 controls show an unwanted hue excursion.

## Translation evidence and existing alternative

The original 36-cell heatmap `vl_1314` has a blue→neutral→tan piecewise scale with domain `[-9,0,9]`. Current native HCL/D50 differs by up to **30 RGB channel units** on its actual cells. Using the existing Lab/D50 interpolation, the equivalent two-endpoint domain `[-9,9]`, and the same three uniformly placed RGB stops gives **0 RGB difference on all 36 original cells**. An additional **16,385 evenly spaced samples across the entire domain** also have 0 rounded RGB difference against the original hue-preserving reference. No observations or source values were changed and no tolerance was widened.

The existing equivalent representation means this source can continue translating. This report makes no library implementation or persisted schema change. Please define and test neutral endpoint hue handling (including neutral↔neutral and long-hue behavior) without changing chromatic endpoint behavior or the existing 8-bit rounding policy. HSL/cubehelix achromatic endpoints may warrant a separate audit rather than silently broadening this fix.

## Problem

In published `ggaction@0.0.19` (`f6b50bf5`), the public range encoding type accepts `lower`/`upper` as `string | { datum: number }`, but constant endpoints fail for a rect. The equivalent public `encodeY` + `encodeY2` operations work and preserve the full-height interval.

## Minimal reproduction

```js
import { chart } from 'ggaction';
const base = () => chart()
  .createCanvas({width:240,height:160,margin:{left:20,right:20,top:20,bottom:20}})
  .createData({values:[{x0:0,x1:2}]})
  .createRectMark({id:'period'})
  .encodeXRange({lower:'x0',upper:'x1',scale:{domain:[0,2],nice:false}});

base().encodeYRange({
  lower:{datum:45000}, upper:{datum:0},
  scale:{domain:[0,45000],nice:false}
});
// TypeError: encodeY field must be a non-empty string.

// Existing public workaround: y=20 and height=120, as expected.
base()
  .encodeY({datum:45000,scale:{domain:[0,45000],nice:false}})
  .encodeY2({datum:0});
```

## Evidence and scope

`src/actions/encodings/ranged.js` initializes endpoints as `{field:args.lower}` / `{field:args.upper}` and prepares datum endpoints only in the area branch. The broad public endpoint union is therefore misleading for other supported ranged mark kinds. Rect direct datum encodings already work; this is not a request to invent observations or add fields for geometry constants.

Found while preserving the two full-height temporal event rectangles in source `vl_0021` (38 population rows and a separate original 2-row event table). The existing two-encoder workaround reproduces the source geometry, so translation can continue. No library implementation or persisted schema change has been made for this report.

Please align the documented/public type scope with supported mark kinds, or make the range wrapper delegate constant endpoints consistently to the existing encoders, with regression tests for unchanged field endpoints and area behavior.

## Problem

`facet` can partition by an original input field and replay a later `fold`, but cannot partition by the new key produced by that fold. An explicit `data` reference does not help. This prevents a compact program from preserving the original wide input, expressing its reshape through the public data action, and faceting the resulting long table.

Reproduced against published **ggaction 0.0.19**, release commit `f6b50bf55a9de1857f106c7aa519326ce7a86d76`.

## Minimal public reproduction

```js
import { chart } from "ggaction";

const p = chart()
  .createCanvas({
    width: 160,
    height: 320,
    margin: { left: 45, right: 10, top: 70, bottom: 50 }
  })
  .createData({ values: [
    { country: "USA", gold: 10, silver: 20 },
    { country: "Canada", gold: 7, silver: 26 }
  ] })
  .createFoldData({
    id: "long", fields: ["gold", "silver"],
    as: { key: "k", value: "v" }
  })
  .createBarPlot({ x: "country", y: "v", guides: false });

p.facet({ field: "k", columns: 2 });
// Facet field "k" is missing from row 0 of partition dataset "data".

p.facet({ field: "k", data: "long", columns: 2 });
// Facet partition dataset "long" is not a common row-preserving
// ancestor of every layer.

// Positive control: supported original-field partition, then local fold replay.
p.facet({ field: "country", columns: 2 });
```

The top margin intentionally provides adequate header space, so both reported failures concern dependency/partition planning rather than layout. The original program remains unchanged after both rejections. Its original input still has two rows and the public fold dataset has four rows.

## Expected capability

An explicit materialized derived table should be usable as a reviewed partition boundary when every visible layer depends on it. For this example the panels must be:

- `gold`: USA 10, Canada 7.
- `silver`: USA 20, Canada 26.

Preserve the original two-row input, declared fold definition, row multiplicity/order, and immutable parent/child lineage. Do not replace them with manually authored long rows or silently partition by a different original field. Existing successful raw-field facet behavior must remain unchanged.

A row-preserving `createTimeUnitData` followed by an explicit derived-data facet already works; the unresolved boundary is the cardinality-changing fold. No new public option name is assumed by this issue. Supporting this requires a concrete dependency/dataflow design and the repository's explicit approval before core implementation.

## Validation needed

Verify parent and child raw/derived tables, exact per-panel marks and key order, descendant replay, immutable failure, edits/rematerialization, shared guides/scales, SVG/PDF/PNG consumers and stored facet contracts. Retain strict ambiguity and unsupported-dependency errors outside the approved boundary.

This blocked a real two-panel translation before any model request. The affected source is held; other chart work continues.
